Four-dimensional scanning transmission electron microscopy (4D-STEM) generates high-dimensional nanobeam electron diffraction (NBED) datasets that contain rich reciprocal-space information. However, extracting physically meaningful information from these datasets remains challenging as conventional analysis relies on established diffraction descriptors that capture only limited aspects of the scattering signal. Although deep generative models offer a promising route for representation learning, their application to experimental NBED datasets with complex microstructural heterogeneity, as well as the physical interpretation of the resulting latent space, remains largely unexplored. Here, the present work develops a reconstruction- only convolutional variational autoencoder (CVAE) to learn compact latent representations directly from experimental NBED data and to systematically investigate the physical significance of the learned latent variables. Building upon this baseline, we developed a physics-informed strain- conditioned CVAE, in which experimentally derived strain maps provide auxiliary supervision during representation learning. Comparative analyses demonstrate that strain supervision fundamentally reorganises the latent manifold while promoting features towards precipitates, interfaces, dislocations, bending, and lattice-distortion-sensitive reciprocal-space features. Latent space visualisation, correlation analysis, latent traversals, and reconstruction diagnostics consistently reveal that the proposed framework learns physically interpretable, microstructure-aware representations while maintaining high-fidelity diffraction reconstruction. The results establish that strain condition acts as a physics-informed regularizer, transforming the latent space from a reconstruction-driven embedding into an interpretable representation of reciprocal-space scattering. This work establishes a general framework for physics-guided and explainable representation learning in 4D-STEM, providing a pathway toward quantitatively interpretable artificial intelligence for electron diffraction and advanced microstructural characterisation.
